# Failures told well: words added to a failure as it travels up, its code
# kept; several failures joined into one; a failure told to a person, in one
# line or as an indented chain; and one dropped on purpose where it cannot
# matter. It is what Rust's `anyhow` gives, said over
# the language's own failure, a `T?` holding a code and a message (spec § 6):
# every function here is generic over any `T?`.
#
# A chain is written into the message. `context(r, what: "reading the
# configuration")` makes the message `reading the configuration: <the old
# message>`, as Go's `fmt.Errorf("...: %w")` and `anyhow`'s `{:#}` write it,
# and keeps the code, so a caller still matches on the code the failure began
# with. Read back, a message is cut into links at each `": "` that stands
# outside double quotes and outside brackets, so a text a message quotes,
# `no "a: b" in "x"`, stays inside one link; the links joined by `": "` give
# the message back, so nothing is lost on the way.
#
# A joined failure's message lists every failure it joins with its code,
# `2 failures (not_found: no "x" in "abc"; invalid: no digit at 3)`, the
# brackets keeping the list one link of a chain; its code is the first
# failure's.
#
# It does not make a failure: the built-in `fail(code:, msg:)` does, and a
# package's codes are its own constants. It holds no backtrace: an abort
# already names its file, line and function, and a failure is told by what its
# chain says, not by where it began. It fails with no code of its own: every
# failure it answers carries a code it was given.

use errors/internal/chains
use errors/internal/samples
use errors/internal/tell
use errors/internal/wrap

## Adding context

# `r` with `what` written before its message where it fails: the message
# becomes `what: <the old message>` and the code stays.
#
# - `r`: the result to add to; a value passes through as it is.
# - `what`: what the program was doing, in words a person reads, such as
#   `reading the configuration`; the empty text still writes its `": "`.
#
# Fails where `r` fails, with its code. It copies the message once. See also
# `context_by`, `with_code`, `chain_of`.
function context<T>(r: T?, what: str) -> T?
    return wrap.context(r, what: what)

# `r` with the words `describe` makes of `subject` written before its message
# where it fails; where `r` holds a value `describe` is not called, so words
# that cost something to build are built only for a failure.
#
# - `r`: the result to add to; a value passes through as it is.
# - `describe`: called once, with `subject`, only where `r` fails; what it
#   answers is written before the message as `context` writes `what`.
# - `subject`: the value the words are made of, such as the path being read.
#
# Fails where `r` fails, with its code. See also `context`.
function context_by<T, A>(r: T?, describe: (function(A) -> str), subject: A) -> T?
    return wrap.context_by(r, describe: describe, subject: subject)

# `r` with `code` in place of its code where it fails; the message stays.
#
# - `r`: the result to recode; a value passes through as it is.
# - `code`: the new code, a constant of the package that fails with it, such
#   as `json.INVALID`; the old code is read nowhere after.
#
# Fails where `r` fails, with `code`. Where the old code says something the
# new one does not, write it into the message with `context` first. See also
# `context`, `has_code`.
function with_code<T>(r: T?, code: str) -> T?
    return wrap.with_code(r, code: code)

## Joining failures

# The failures of `results` joined into one, or `ok()` where none fails.
#
# - `results`: the results to join, in order; an empty list, or one whose
#   every result holds a value, gives `ok()`. Results of several types are
#   joined by passing each through `discard_value` first.
#
# One failure is answered as it is, its code and message unchanged. Several
# give one failure whose code is the first one's and whose message is `<n>
# failures (<code>: <message>; ...)`, in the order of `results`, which
# `parts_of` reads back and `has_code` searches.
#
# Fails where any result fails, as above. See also `collect`, `parts_of`.
function combine<T>(results: [T?]) -> ()?
    return wrap.combine(results)

# Every value of `results` in order, where none fails; else their failures
# joined as `combine` joins them.
#
# - `results`: the results to read; an empty list gives an empty list.
#
# Fails where any result fails: with that failure as it is where it is the only
# one, else with the joined failure `combine` answers. See also `combine`, and
# `lists.all_ok`, which stops at the first failure and answers it alone.
function collect<T>(results: [T?]) -> [T]?
    return wrap.collect(results)

# `r` with its value dropped: `ok()` where it holds one, the same failure where
# it fails.
#
# - `r`: the result to read.
#
# It is how results of several types go into one `[()?]` for `combine`. Fails
# where `r` fails, with its code and its message. See also `combine`.
function discard_value<T>(r: T?) -> ()?
    return wrap.discard_value(r)

## Dropping a failure

# Nothing: the failure `r` may hold is dropped, for a failure that cannot
# change what the program does next.
#
# - `r`: the result of a call made for its effect alone, such as closing a
#   stream. It is a `()?`, so no value is dropped with the failure: a call
#   that answers a value, or a list or a record holding failures of their
#   own, is read by the program, and where its value truly does not matter
#   `discard_value` says so first, in words a reader sees.
#
# It is for a failure that genuinely cannot matter: a stream closed on the
# way out of a failure already being answered, a scratch folder removed that
# may already be gone, a line written to standard error, which has nowhere
# else to tell its own failure. A failure the program acts on or tells is
# answered with `?`, `match`, `.default` or `context`, never through this.
# It decides nothing: where `_ = r.is_err()` asks a question and throws the
# answer away, its name says the drop is meant. See also `discard_value`.
function ignore(r: ()?)
    wrap.ignore(r)

## Reading a failure

# The links of the message of `r`, the outermost first: what each `context`
# wrote, then the message the failure began with.
#
# - `r`: the result to read; a value has no chain and gives an empty list.
#
# The message is cut at each `": "` outside double quotes and outside round
# or square brackets, so `chain_of(r).join(": ")` is the message again, and a
# link is empty where the message holds `": "` twice in a row. The last link
# is the root, `anyhow`'s `root_cause`. A quote or a bracket that does not
# close keeps the rest of the message in one link. See also `context`,
# `format_chain`.
function chain_of<T>(r: T?) -> [str]
    return chains.chain_of(r)

# The failures a joined failure holds, each with its own code and message; a
# failure that joins nothing is its own one part.
#
# - `r`: the result to read; a value gives an empty list.
#
# The parts are read from the last link of the chain, where `combine` writes
# them, in their order; the context written before that link is in no part.
# A link that only looks joined, whose count differs from its parts or one of
# whose parts has no code, makes `r` one part. See also `combine`,
# `has_code`.
function parts_of<T>(r: T?) -> [()?]
    return chains.parts_of(r)

# Whether `r` fails with `code`, or joins, at any depth, a failure that does.
#
# - `r`: the result to ask; a value answers `false`.
# - `code`: the code to look for, such as `fs.NOT_FOUND`.
#
# It reads each joined part once, on a stack of its own. See also
# `parts_of`, `with_code`.
function has_code<T>(r: T?, code: str) -> bool
    return chains.has_code(r, code: code)

## Telling a failure

# The failure of `r` told in one line as an abort tells it, `<code>:
# <message>`.
#
# - `r`: the result to tell; a value has nothing to tell and gives the empty
#   text.
#
# A control character in the code or the message, a line break included, is
# written as a literal of the language writes it, `\n`, `\t`, `\u{1b}`, so the
# line stays one line and moves no terminal's cursor. See also
# `format_chain`.
function format_line<T>(r: T?) -> str
    return tell.format_line(r)

# The failure of `r` told as an indented chain, one link a line: its code and
# its outermost link first, then each link that caused it.
#
# - `r`: the result to tell; a value gives the empty text.
#
# The first line reads `<code>: <first link>`, and each further link stands
# four spaces in after `caused by: `. A joined failure's link reads `<n>
# failures`, and each part follows four spaces further in, told the same way.
# The lines are joined by a line feed, with none after the last; control
# characters are written as `format_line` writes them. See also
# `format_line`, `chain_of`.
function format_chain<T>(r: T?) -> str
    return tell.format_chain(r)

## Examples

# Context added on the way up: the message grows, the code stays.
test "context: a missing key read while loading a configuration"
    r: i64? = fail(code: "not_found", msg: "no \"port\" in \"server\"")

    match context(r, what: "reading the configuration")
        .ok _ => assert false
        .err e =>
            assert e.code == "not_found"
            assert e.msg == "reading the configuration: no \"port\" in \"server\""

# Context made by a function, only where the result fails; `samples.reading`
# is `function reading(path: str) -> str`, answering `f"reading \"{path}\""`.
test "context_by: the path being read named in the failure"
    r: str? = fail(code: "file_not_found", msg: "no such file")
    told = format_line(context_by(r, describe: samples.reading, subject: "app.toml"))
    assert told == "file_not_found: reading \"app.toml\": no such file"

# A failure given the code of the package that hands it on.
test "with_code: a missing file told as a configuration that is not valid"
    r: i64? = fail(code: "file_not_found", msg: "no such file")
    assert format_line(with_code(r, code: "invalid")) == "invalid: no such file"

# Every failure of a list joined into one.
test "combine: two of three checks fail"
    checks: [()?] = [
        ok()
        fail(code: "invalid", msg: "no digit at 3")
        fail(code: "not_found", msg: "no \"x\" in \"abc\"")
    ]

    match combine(checks)
        .ok _ => assert false
        .err e =>
            assert e.code == "invalid"
            assert e.msg == "2 failures (invalid: no digit at 3; not_found: no \"x\" in \"abc\")"

# The values of a list of results, all of which hold one.
test "collect: three numbers read"
    read: [i64?] = [ok(1), ok(2), ok(3)]
    assert collect(read).must() == [1, 2, 3]

# A number's failure put beside a text's, for `combine`.
test "discard_value: two results of two types joined"
    count: i64? = fail(code: "invalid", msg: "not a number")
    name: str? = ok("ada")
    joined = combine([discard_value(count), discard_value(name)])
    assert format_line(joined) == "invalid: not a number"

# A stream's close dropped on the way out of a failure the caller is told:
# the close cannot change what the caller does, the read's failure can.
test "ignore: a close's failure dropped, the read's own answered"
    read: str? = fail(code: "closed", msg: "the peer closed the connection")
    closing: ()? = fail(code: "closed", msg: "already closed")
    ignore(closing)
    assert format_line(context(read, what: "reading the request")) == "closed: reading the request: the peer closed the connection"

# The links of a chain, the outermost first.
test "chain_of: two contexts over a quoted message"
    r: i64? = fail(code: "not_found", msg: "no \"a: b\" in \"x\"")
    told = context(context(r, what: "opening \"x.toml\""), what: "reading the configuration")
    assert chain_of(told) == ["reading the configuration", "opening \"x.toml\"", "no \"a: b\" in \"x\""]

# The failures a joined failure holds, read back.
test "parts_of: the two failures of a joined one"
    a: ()? = fail(code: "invalid", msg: "no digit at 3")
    b: ()? = fail(code: "not_found", msg: "no \"x\"")
    assert parts_of(combine([a, b])) == [a, b]

# Asking a joined failure for a code its second part has.
test "has_code: a code found inside a joined failure"
    failures: [()?] = [fail(code: "invalid", msg: "a"), fail(code: "not_found", msg: "b")]
    joined = combine(failures)
    assert has_code(joined, code: "not_found")
    assert !has_code(joined, code: "exists")

# A failure told in one line.
test "format_line: a failure with its context"
    r: i64? = fail(code: "not_found", msg: "no \"port\"")
    assert format_line(context(r, what: "reading the configuration")) == "not_found: reading the configuration: no \"port\""

# A failure told as an indented chain.
test "format_chain: a failure with two contexts"
    r: i64? = fail(code: "not_found", msg: "no \"port\"")
    told = format_chain(context(context(r, what: "opening \"app.toml\""), what: "starting the server"))
    assert told == "not_found: starting the server\n    caused by: opening \"app.toml\"\n    caused by: no \"port\""
